1
00:00:05,489 --> 00:00:13,539
Exchange data over FHIR — Clinical ▸ FHIR — The standard your partners speak — and the worklist for when a message does not land.

2
00:00:19,069 --> 00:00:26,184
Clinical ▸ FHIR Resources is the clinic’s data in the format the rest of healthcare reads.

3
00:00:26,184 --> 00:00:35,391
FHIR R4 is the standard. A patient is a Patient, a vital sign is an Observation, a diagnosis is a Condition.

4
00:00:35,391 --> 00:00:44,978
Which matters because a partner system does not need to know anything about AureonCare to read it.

5
00:00:44,978 --> 00:01:00,555
Filter by resource type to see just the diagnoses, just the observations, just the medications.

6
00:01:00,555 --> 00:01:11,498
Sync pushes that patient’s record out as FHIR resources, and stamps the version it sent.

7
00:01:11,498 --> 00:01:22,887
A bundle is the whole patient in one file — what you hand to a specialist or a new provider.

8
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:30,446
FHIR Tracking is the worklist that matters day to day: outbound exchanges that need someone to act.

9
00:01:30,446 --> 00:01:42,230
A prescription that the pharmacy never acknowledged. A lab order the lab rejected. Each with a tracking number.

10
00:01:42,230 --> 00:01:50,198
Open one and it tells you what actually went wrong — here, the ordering provider has no NPI on file.

11
00:01:50,198 --> 00:01:58,111
And it tells you what to do: fix the provider record, then resend. No new order, no phone call yet.

12
00:01:58,111 --> 00:02:06,064
Severity separates the two kinds: an error needs a person, a warning usually clears on a retry.

13
00:02:06,064 --> 00:02:13,340
Check this list daily. It is the difference between finding out here, and finding out when a patient calls.

14
00:02:15,299 --> 00:02:32,029
What you just did

15
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:41,662
